{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Adam.</b> Implying man, woman, child, or little girl." "<b>In the skin of his flesh.</b> Since it is about to say further on ״a plague on the head or on the beard,״ it was necessary to state here: in the skin of his flesh." "<b>Se'et.</b> An expression of elevation — like the appearance of a shadow, which is higher than the appearance of the sun." "<b>Sapachat.</b> Secondary to se'et, and secondary to baheret. It has offshoots of se'et, and also offshoots of baheret, to combine with it — as in ״ספחני נא אל אחת הכהונות״ (attach me now to one of the priesthoods)." "<b>For a plague of tzara'at.</b> That the place of the plague be white flesh — as it is written, ״מצורעת כשלג״ (white as snow), and it is written ״ומראה הנגע עמק״ — meaning: white." "<b>And he shall be brought to Aharon the priest.</b> Even against his will. <b>Or to one of his sons.</b> Even those with blemishes." "<b>Achad.</b> The alef with patach." "<b>The priests.</b> And not disqualified priests (chalalim). And from where do we include all Israel? It says <b>or to one</b> — to include all Israel. If so, why does it say 'of his sons, the priests'? To teach that there is no declaration of impurity or purity except from the mouth of a priest. How so? A sage among Israel examines the lesion and says to the priest, even though he is an ignoramus, 'Say: it is impure,' and it is impure; 'Say: it is pure,' and it is pure."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:11:51.365071189Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}